window.CELPIP_LESSONS = {"lessons":[{"id":"LS-L1","skill":"listening","part":"Part 1: Listening to Problem Solving","title":"Following a Problem and Its Fix","whatItTests":"You follow a conversation in which someone describes a personal problem and the speakers work toward a solution.","strategy":["Before listening, glance at the answer choices so you know whether to focus on the cause, the options or the final decision.","Note who has the problem and who is offering help; the roles often explain the rest.","Listen for how the problem is described first, then for each suggestion that follows.","Pay attention to the speaker's reaction to each idea, since agreement or doubt shows which fix is chosen.","Jot two or three keywords only, such as the problem, one option and the outcome."],"pitfalls":["Choosing the first solution mentioned when the speakers later reject it.","Writing full sentences while the audio keeps going and missing the next point.","Mixing up the person who has the problem with the person giving advice."],"howToPractice":"Listen to short everyday advice conversations, pausing after each to say aloud what the problem was and what was decided. Then replay it and check which details you missed.","structure":""},{"id":"LS-L2","skill":"listening","part":"Part 2: Listening to a Daily Life Conversation","title":"Everyday Chat Between Two People","whatItTests":"You understand a normal conversation between friends, coworkers or family members about daily matters.","strategy":["Decide quickly who the speakers are and how they know each other, because that sets the tone.","Listen for the main reason the conversation is happening before worrying about details.","Notice casual phrases and short replies such as \"sure thing\" or \"I guess so\", which carry attitude.","Pay attention to changes of topic; a new topic often brings a new question.","Predict the type of answer (who, what, when, why) from the question wording."],"pitfalls":["Taking casual or joking remarks literally.","Fixing on one unfamiliar word instead of following the overall meaning.","Choosing an answer because it repeats a word you heard, even when the meaning differs."],"howToPractice":"Watch or listen to short natural conversations, such as sitcom clips or casual podcasts, and summarize each in one sentence. Practise recognizing contractions and reduced speech like \"gonna\" and \"wanna\".","structure":""},{"id":"LS-L3","skill":"listening","part":"Part 3: Listening for Information","title":"Picking Out Plans and Service Details","whatItTests":"You pick out specific information from a longer conversation about things like plans or services.","strategy":["Read the choices first and mark which kind of detail is needed: a name, a place, a time, a cost or a condition.","Listen for corrections, such as \"actually\" or \"sorry, I meant\", because the final version is the one that counts.","Keep a quick list of the main items discussed so later questions are easier to place.","Notice comparisons between options and who prefers which.","Stay calm if you miss one detail and keep listening for the next one."],"pitfalls":["Writing down the first number or date you hear and ignoring a later correction.","Losing track of which option belongs to which speaker.","Dwelling on a missed answer and then missing the next one too."],"howToPractice":"Practise with recorded dialogues about bookings, appointments or trip planning. After each one, write the key facts from memory in a short list and compare them with the audio.","structure":""},{"id":"LS-L4","skill":"listening","part":"Part 4: Listening to a News Item","title":"Catching the Facts in a News Report","whatItTests":"You understand the key facts in a short radio-style news report.","strategy":["Listen for the five basics: what happened, who was involved, where, when and why it matters.","Expect the main event first and supporting detail afterwards.","Watch for words that signal cause, result or what happens next.","Note numbers, places and names briefly, using symbols or initials to save time.","Check each answer choice against what was actually reported, not what you assume about the topic."],"pitfalls":["Adding outside knowledge about the topic instead of using the report.","Confusing the reporter's statements with a quote from someone interviewed.","Mishearing similar numbers, such as thirteen and thirty, and not using context to check."],"howToPractice":"Listen to short radio news items and tell yourself the story back in three sentences. Repeat with the same clip later to see what you now catch.","structure":""},{"id":"LS-L5","skill":"listening","part":"Part 5: Listening to a Discussion","title":"Keeping Up With an Academic Discussion","whatItTests":"You follow a more formal discussion between two or more speakers on an academic-style topic.","strategy":["Identify the topic in the first few sentences and keep it in mind as the anchor.","Track each speaker's main point separately, using initials or labels in your notes.","Listen for signals of agreement, disagreement and examples.","Expect more abstract vocabulary; guess meaning from the words around it.","Choose answers that fit the whole discussion rather than a single remark."],"pitfalls":["Attributing one speaker's idea to another.","Stopping to work out a hard word and falling behind.","Picking an answer that is true of the topic generally but was not said."],"howToPractice":"Listen to short educational talks or panel discussions and note the topic and each speaker's main point. Then explain the discussion aloud to a friend or to yourself.","structure":""},{"id":"LS-L6","skill":"listening","part":"Part 6: Listening to Viewpoints","title":"Separating Two Opposing Opinions","whatItTests":"You tell apart two speakers who hold opposing views, with the recording played twice.","strategy":["On the first listening, work out each speaker's overall position.","On the second listening, check the reasons and examples behind each position.","Use two columns in your notes, one for each speaker.","Listen for opinion language such as \"I believe\", \"in my view\" and \"I disagree\".","Match each answer choice to the right speaker before choosing it."],"pitfalls":["Treating a point one speaker mentions only to argue against it as that speaker's own view.","Listening to the second playing passively instead of using it to confirm details.","Mixing up the two voices when they sound similar."],"howToPractice":"Find short debates or opinion segments and write one line for each side after hearing them. Replay once and add a reason for each side, as you would on a second listening.","structure":""},{"id":"LS-R1","skill":"reading","part":"Part 1: Reading Correspondence","title":"Making Sense of an Email or Letter","whatItTests":"You read an email or letter and either fill in blanks or answer questions about its content.","strategy":["Identify who is writing to whom and why before reading the details.","Notice the tone, such as polite, urgent or friendly, because it affects word choice.","For blanks, read the sentences before and after to see which word fits grammatically and in meaning.","Look for the request or action the writer wants.","Reread your choice in the sentence to check that it sounds natural."],"pitfalls":["Choosing a word that fits the sentence alone but not the whole message.","Ignoring grammar clues like verb tense or whether a noun is singular or plural.","Missing a polite request that is worded indirectly."],"howToPractice":"Read sample emails and letters and say in one sentence what the writer wants. Then cover a few words and try to guess the missing ones from context.","structure":""},{"id":"LS-R2","skill":"reading","part":"Part 2: Reading to Apply a Diagram","title":"Using a Notice, Schedule or Diagram","whatItTests":"You read a notice, schedule or diagram and use its information to answer practical questions.","strategy":["Study the layout first: headings, columns, labels and symbols.","Read each question carefully and underline the conditions, such as dates, groups or limits.","Locate the matching part of the document and check every condition against it.","Watch for exceptions, footnotes and small print.","Use the process of elimination for choices that break any one condition."],"pitfalls":["Answering from the first matching row without checking the other conditions.","Overlooking exceptions or notes at the bottom.","Reading the question too quickly and answering a slightly different one."],"howToPractice":"Practise with real timetables, event flyers, forms and floor plans. Make up your own questions that need two or three pieces of information to answer.","structure":""},{"id":"LS-R3","skill":"reading","part":"Part 3: Reading for Information","title":"Understanding a Longer Informational Text","whatItTests":"You read a longer passage that gives information and answer questions about details and what is implied.","strategy":["Skim the headings and the first line of each paragraph to learn the structure.","Read the questions before the full text so you know what to look for.","Mark where each answer is found, then reread that section closely.","For inference questions, choose what the text strongly suggests, not what it merely allows.","Look for paraphrases; correct choices often restate the passage in new words."],"pitfalls":["Relying on your own knowledge of the topic instead of the text.","Choosing an option that copies words from the passage but changes the meaning.","Reading every sentence slowly and running short on time."],"howToPractice":"Read short articles and write a one-line summary of each paragraph. Then write two questions of your own, one detail and one inference, and answer them.","structure":""},{"id":"LS-R4","skill":"reading","part":"Part 4: Reading for Viewpoints","title":"Who Thinks What in a Viewpoint Article","whatItTests":"You read an article with several viewpoints and work out which person holds which opinion.","strategy":["Note each person's name as it appears and write a few words about their position.","Look for opinion verbs such as argues, claims, worries and supports.","Separate facts and background from opinions.","Check whether a person agrees with an idea or is only describing it.","Match answer choices to the right person before choosing."],"pitfalls":["Giving one person's opinion to someone mentioned nearby.","Treating the writer's background explanation as a speaker's view.","Missing a change of position signalled by words like \"but\" or \"however\"."],"howToPractice":"Read opinion pieces or letters to the editor and make a small table of names and positions. Then rewrite each position in your own words.","structure":""},{"id":"LS-W1","skill":"writing","part":"Task 1: Writing an Email","title":"Writing a Clear Email","whatItTests":"You write an email that responds to a given situation and covers everything the prompt asks for.","strategy":["Underline every point the prompt asks you to cover and tick them off as you write.","Decide the tone first: formal for a manager or company, friendly for someone you know well.","Plan a simple order of ideas in a few notes before writing full sentences.","Give specific details, such as a reason, a date or a request, rather than vague statements.","Leave time at the end to proofread grammar, spelling and completeness."],"pitfalls":["Skipping one of the points in the prompt.","Using a casual tone with someone you should address formally, or the reverse.","Writing a long opening and running out of time for the closing."],"howToPractice":"Write practice emails to prompts you invent from everyday life, such as asking for a refund or inviting a neighbour. Compare each with your checklist of required points and revise it once.","structure":"Greeting: address the reader suitably.\nOpening: state why you are writing.\nBody: one short paragraph for each required point, with a reason or detail.\nClosing: say what you hope will happen next, then sign off."},{"id":"LS-W2","skill":"writing","part":"Task 2: Responding to Survey Questions","title":"Giving and Defending an Opinion","whatItTests":"You answer a survey-style question by stating an opinion and supporting it with reasons.","strategy":["State your choice clearly in the first sentence.","Choose two or three strong reasons rather than many weak ones.","Support each reason with a brief example from daily life.","Use linking words such as \"for instance\", \"as a result\" and \"on the other hand\".","Finish with a sentence that restates your position."],"pitfalls":["Changing position partway through the response.","Listing reasons without explaining or giving examples.","Repeating the same word or phrase because no alternatives were prepared."],"howToPractice":"Answer simple opinion questions in short paragraphs, each with a position, two reasons and a closing line. Afterwards underline repeated words and replace some with precise alternatives.","structure":"Opening: state your opinion directly.\nReason 1: explain it with a short example.\nReason 2: explain it with a short example.\nClosing: restate your opinion in different words."},{"id":"LS-S1","skill":"speaking","part":"Task 1: Giving Advice","title":"Advising Someone","whatItTests":"You give helpful advice to a person based on a situation that is described to you.","strategy":["Identify the person and the problem in your notes during preparation.","Offer two or three concrete suggestions, each with a reason.","Use advice language such as \"I would recommend\", \"you could\" and \"it might help to\".","Speak directly to the person, with a warm and supportive tone.","Finish with an encouraging closing remark."],"pitfalls":["Giving only one idea and then running out of things to say.","Describing the problem again instead of offering solutions.","Using a tone that sounds bossy rather than helpful."],"howToPractice":"Ask a friend for small everyday problems, or invent your own, and give spoken advice. Record yourself and check that each suggestion has a reason.","structure":"Greet and show you understand the situation.\nSuggestion 1 with a reason.\nSuggestion 2 with a reason.\nEncourage the person and wish them well."},{"id":"LS-S2","skill":"speaking","part":"Task 2: Talking About a Personal Experience","title":"Telling a Personal Experience","whatItTests":"You tell a story from your own life that relates to the topic you are given.","strategy":["Choose an experience you can talk about easily, even if it is small.","Use preparation time to note the setting, what happened and how it ended.","Use past tense forms and time words like \"first\", \"then\" and \"afterwards\".","Add a few sensory or emotional details to make the story vivid.","Close by saying what you learned or how you felt."],"pitfalls":["Picking a dramatic story that needs vocabulary you do not have.","Staying in the present tense when telling a past event.","Stopping after the events without saying why they mattered."],"howToPractice":"Tell short stories aloud about a trip, a lesson or a surprise, using a simple three-point outline. Record them and check your past tense forms.","structure":"Set the scene: when, where and who.\nTell what happened in order.\nSay how it ended.\nShare what you learned or how you felt."},{"id":"LS-S3","skill":"speaking","part":"Task 3: Describing a Scene","title":"Describing a Scene","whatItTests":"You describe a pictured scene clearly and in detail.","strategy":["Start with the big picture, such as the place and the general mood.","Then move in an order, such as foreground to background or left to right.","Use position words like \"on the left\", \"behind\" and \"next to\".","Describe what people are doing using the present continuous.","Add small details such as colours, clothing and expressions to fill the time."],"pitfalls":["Naming objects in a random list without any order.","Describing only one corner of the picture.","Guessing at things you cannot see instead of describing what is there."],"howToPractice":"Practise with photos from magazines or your phone, describing each one aloud in an organized way. Record yourself and see whether the listener could picture it without seeing it.","structure":"Overview: where this is and what is happening.\nMain subjects and what they are doing.\nSurroundings and background details.\nClose with the overall feeling of the scene."},{"id":"LS-S4","skill":"speaking","part":"Task 4: Making Predictions","title":"Predicting What Happens Next","whatItTests":"You say what is likely to happen next in a pictured scene and explain why.","strategy":["Begin by briefly saying what is happening now.","Use clues in the picture to support each prediction.","Use future language such as \"will\", \"is going to\" and \"is likely to\".","Give two or three predictions, ranging from the most to the least likely.","Link each prediction to a reason with \"because\" or \"since\"."],"pitfalls":["Describing the scene for so long that no time remains for predictions.","Making predictions that have no connection to anything in the picture.","Using only \"will\" in every sentence."],"howToPractice":"Look at photos of events in progress and say aloud what might happen in the next few minutes. Then practise using three different future structures.","structure":"Briefly describe the current situation.\nPrediction 1 with a clue from the picture.\nPrediction 2 with a reason.\nA final guess about the outcome."},{"id":"LS-S5","skill":"speaking","part":"Task 5: Comparing and Persuading","title":"Comparing Options and Persuading","whatItTests":"You compare two options and try to convince someone to choose one of them.","strategy":["Write both options in your notes with two or three points for each.","Decide on your choice at once and keep to it.","Compare the options on the same points so the contrast is clear.","Use persuasive phrases such as \"the main advantage is\" and \"you will really enjoy\".","End by repeating your recommendation."],"pitfalls":["Switching your recommendation halfway through.","Talking about only one option.","Giving a list of features without saying why they matter to the listener."],"howToPractice":"Compare two everyday choices, such as two phones or two weekend plans, and try to persuade a friend. Record it and check that each point is tied to a benefit.","structure":"Introduce both options.\nCompare them on two or three points.\nState which you recommend and why.\nClose with a friendly appeal."},{"id":"LS-S6","skill":"speaking","part":"Task 6: Dealing with a Difficult Situation","title":"Handling an Awkward Situation Politely","whatItTests":"You respond tactfully to an awkward or difficult scenario.","strategy":["Identify who you are speaking to and what outcome you want.","Open with a polite or understanding phrase to lower tension.","Explain the problem calmly without blaming.","Offer a solution or compromise.","Keep your tone respectful, even when you are saying no."],"pitfalls":["Sounding blunt or accusing.","Apologizing repeatedly without proposing anything.","Using vocabulary that is too informal for the listener."],"howToPractice":"Role-play difficult conversations with a friend, such as returning a late item or declining an invitation. Practise polite softening phrases like \"I understand, however\".","structure":"Open politely and show understanding.\nExplain the issue or your position.\nOffer a solution or alternative.\nClose with thanks or goodwill."},{"id":"LS-S7","skill":"speaking","part":"Task 7: Expressing Opinions","title":"Stating and Supporting an Opinion","whatItTests":"You give your opinion on a topic and support it with reasons.","strategy":["State your opinion in the first sentence.","Choose two reasons that you can explain easily.","Add a short example from real life for each reason.","Use opinion phrases like \"in my view\" and \"I strongly believe\".","Close by restating your position."],"pitfalls":["Trying to present both sides and never taking a clear position.","Giving reasons without examples.","Using the same sentence pattern throughout."],"howToPractice":"Give one-minute spoken opinions on everyday topics. Listen back and note whether each reason has an example.","structure":"Opinion: say your view clearly.\nReason 1 with an example.\nReason 2 with an example.\nConclusion: restate your view."},{"id":"LS-S8","skill":"speaking","part":"Task 8: Describing an Unusual Situation","title":"Describing Something Unusual","whatItTests":"You describe an unusual or surprising image in detail.","strategy":["Start by naming what is surprising or out of place.","Then describe the whole scene in an orderly way.","Use comparison phrases such as \"it looks like\" and \"it seems as though\".","Mention how people in the image might be reacting.","Fill the time with specific details such as shapes, colours and positions."],"pitfalls":["Spending the whole time saying the picture is strange without describing it.","Making up a long story instead of describing what is visible.","Pausing for long stretches while searching for the exact word."],"howToPractice":"Find odd or surprising pictures and describe them aloud. When a word is missing, practise describing around it, for example \"a tool used for cutting\".","structure":"Say what is unusual about the picture.\nDescribe the main subject and its surroundings.\nDescribe other details and people's reactions.\nEnd with your overall impression."}],"studyTips":[{"area":"notes","tip":"Use short keywords and symbols instead of full sentences so you can keep listening or reading."},{"area":"notes","tip":"Divide your notes page into columns or labels for different speakers, options or topics."},{"area":"notes","tip":"After each practice, add one line to your notes about what you would do differently next time."},{"area":"review","tip":"Keep a mistake log with the question, your answer, the right answer and why you went wrong."},{"area":"review","tip":"Review your mistake log after one day, then after a few days, then after a week."},{"area":"review","tip":"Group your errors by type, such as grammar, vocabulary or missed details, and work on the most common type first."},{"area":"vocabulary","tip":"Learn new words in phrases or sentences instead of single words, so you remember how they are used."},{"area":"vocabulary","tip":"Keep a list of useful linking and opinion phrases and use one or two new ones in each practice."},{"area":"vocabulary","tip":"Collect words from real emails, news and conversations, then use each one in a sentence of your own."},{"area":"timed","tip":"Practise with a timer so that working within limits feels normal."},{"area":"timed","tip":"Decide ahead of time what you will do when stuck: make your best guess and move on."},{"area":"timed","tip":"Practise a full session under test-like conditions now and then, without pausing or replaying anything."}]};
